<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Kleidion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1014">1014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Clidium, Battle of Belasitsa · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-conquest-of-bulgaria">Byzantine conquest of Bulgaria</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Kleidion was fought on 29 July 1014 in the Belasitsa mountains, where the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> outflanked and destroyed the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Samuel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. According to Byzantine chroniclers, Basil had some 15,000 prisoners blinded, leaving one man in every hundred with one eye to lead them home, and Samuel died of shock soon after. Bulgaria was fully annexed by 1018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kleidion.jpg" alt="Battle of Kleidion" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: from the Middle Ages, unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SamuilsDeathBGhistory.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The conflict began in the 7th century, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> under Khan Asparukh set up a state along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> inside a Byzantine province. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> in 971, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> came under Byzantine rule, but the western lands stayed autonomous under the Comitopuli brothers, including Samuel. Basil II took the throne in 976 intent on destroying independent Bulgaria, and Samuel's army annihilated his in the Gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> pass in 986.</p><p>Samuel was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-spercheios">Battle of Spercheios</a> in 996 after invading southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. From 1000 Basil took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a> in 1003 and defeated Samuel at Skopie the next year, campaigning every year and slowly depriving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> of their strongholds. In 1014 Samuel resolved to stop the Byzantine army before it entered the Bulgarian heartland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Samuel dug ditches and fortified the passes, above all the pass of Kleidion on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Struma</a> river, and he gathered a large army, which some claimed numbered as many as 45,000. A thick wooden wall, defended by 15,000 to 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, stopped Basil's army near the village of Klyuch, and its first assault was repulsed with heavy losses.</p><p>Basil kept attacking the wall while the general Nikephoros Xiphias led troops along a steep path around Belasitsa mountain. On 29 July Xiphias fell on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> from behind, trapping them in the valley. Basil broke through and destroyed the wall. Thousands were killed in the rout. Samuel barely escaped when his son Gabriel Radomir put him on his own horse and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>John Skylitzes records that Basil took 15,000 prisoners, though modern historians such as Vasil Zlatarski consider such numbers exaggerated. Basil blinded 99 men in each group of 100 and left one with a single eye to lead the others home, partly in retaliation for the death of his favourite general Theophylact Botaneiates, who was ambushed and killed soon after the battle. Basil gained the name Boulgaroktonos, the Bulgar-slayer. Samuel died of a heart attack on 6 October 1014, reportedly on seeing his blinded soldiers.</p><p>The war continued for four more years, and some modern historians doubt that the defeat was as complete as Skylitzes and Kekaumenos describe. Others stress that Samuel's death mattered more. His heirs Gabriel Radomir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-vladislav">Ivan Vladislav</a> could not resist Basil, and many governors surrendered voluntarily. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> was defeated in 1018 and remained a Byzantine province until the uprising of the Asen brothers in 1185. The Byzantine frontier returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> for the first time since the 7th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Kleidion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Samuel the western Bulgarians defeated Basil II at the Gates of Trajan in 986, but were crushed at <b>Kleidion</b> in 1014.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
